---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Navbar from '../components/Navbar.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import LegalPage from '../components/LegalPage.astro';

/**
 * Written from what the site actually does, not from a template.
 *
 * Every claim below was checked against the code: lib/contact.ts and
 * lib/estimate.ts for what the forms send, lib/supabase.ts for where it
 * goes, BaseLayout for storage and third-party requests. A policy that
 * describes collection a site does not do — or omits collection it does —
 * is worse than no policy, because it is a documented promise nobody
 * checked.
 *
 * TODO(airaf): two things here need a decision rather than a draft.
 *   1. Retention. The text says leads are kept while a conversation is
 *      live and reviewed periodically. Give it a real period and this
 *      becomes a commitment instead of a description.
 *   2. Supabase region. If the project is not hosted in the EU, EU/UK
 *      enquiries involve an international transfer and that should be
 *      named explicitly rather than implied.
 */
const updated = '2026-09-05';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy Policy — Xtenzium"
  description="What Xtenzium collects when you use this site, why, where it is stored, and how to have it removed. No cookies, no analytics, no tracking."
  path="/privacy"
>
  <Navbar />

  <main id="main">
    <LegalPage
      eyebrow="Privacy"
      title="What we collect, and what we don't."
      summary="Short, because there is little to describe. This site sets no cookies and sends nothing to an advertising network. We count visits ourselves, and the only personal data we hold is what you type into a form."
      updated={updated}
    >
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p>
        We collect the details you enter into the contact form or the project
        estimator, and a count of which pages get read. There are no cookies on
        this site, no advertising pixels, and no third-party analytics — no
        Google Analytics, no Tag Manager, nothing that follows you to another
        site. Nothing is sold, rented, or shared for marketing.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Xtenzium</strong> is a digital agency based in Karachi, Sindh,
        Pakistan, and is the controller of the data described here. Reach us
        about anything on this page at{' '}
        <a href="mailto:contact@xtenzium.com">contact@xtenzium.com</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What we collect</h2>
      <dl>
        <dt>Contact form</dt>
        <dd>
          Your name, email address, and message, which are required; and your
          company, budget range and the service you are asking about, which are
          optional and blank unless you fill them in.
        </dd>

        <dt>Project estimator</dt>
        <dd>
          Your email address, the four answers you selected, and the range the
          estimator produced. The answers are ranges and categories rather than
          anything identifying — the estimator asks nothing about you until it
          has already shown you a number.
        </dd>

        <dt>Page analytics</dt>
        <dd>
          Which pages are viewed, how far down them people read, which outbound
          links get clicked, and whether a form was started and finished. It is
          recorded by us, into our own database, and never leaves it.
          <br /><br />
          Each visit is tagged with a random identifier generated in your
          browser and held in session storage. It is not a cookie, it is not
          derived from anything about you, and it is discarded when you close
          the tab — so the same browser tomorrow is a new visit and cannot be
          connected to this one. It exists so that ten pages read by one person
          are not counted as ten people.
          <br /><br />
          If you send an enquiry, that identifier is stored alongside it, which
          is how we learn which pages lead to conversations. That link is only
          as durable as the session it came from.
          <br /><br />
          If your browser sends a Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control
          signal, none of this runs at all. Neither signal legally binds us
          here; ignoring an explicit request while claiming to be the
          privacy-respecting option is not a position worth holding.
        </dd>

        <dt>Server logs</dt>
        <dd>
          Our host records ordinary request information, including IP address,
          browser user agent and the page requested. This is standard web
          server logging, used to keep the site available and to investigate
          abuse. We do not build profiles from it.
        </dd>
      </dl>

      <p>
        We do not ask for, and have no use for, special category data — health,
        political opinions, biometrics and the rest. Please do not send it
        through these forms.
      </p>

      <h2>Why we hold it</h2>
      <p>
        To reply to you. An enquiry is a request to be contacted back, and the
        details you send are used to answer it and to carry on that
        conversation — quoting, scoping, and delivering work if it gets that
        far. In data protection terms this is our legitimate interest in
        responding to people who deliberately contact a business, and the steps
        necessary before entering a contract at your request.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not send marketing email. If that ever changes, it will be
        something you opt into rather than something you are enrolled in by
        having once made an enquiry.
      </p>

      <h2>Where it goes</h2>
      <p>
        Form submissions are stored in a Supabase database that only we can read
        — the public site can write an enquiry and cannot read any of them
        back. Access is limited to the people at Xtenzium who need it to reply
        to you.
      </p>
      <p>Three third parties are involved in running this site:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Supabase</strong> — the database that stores enquiries, acting
          as our processor.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Vercel</strong> — hosting and delivery, which involves the
          server logging described above.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Google Fonts</strong> — the site loads three typefaces from
          Google's servers, which means your browser makes a request to Google
          and your IP address is visible to them as part of it. No cookie is
          set by this and we receive nothing from it, but it is a request to a
          third party and you are entitled to know it happens.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Xtenzium is in Pakistan, so an enquiry sent from the UK or the EU
        involves a transfer outside that jurisdiction. If you would rather not
        use the form for that reason, email is fine, and so is asking us to
        delete the record after we have replied.
      </p>

      <h2>Cookies and browser storage</h2>
      <p>
        This site sets <strong>no cookies</strong>. There is no consent banner
        because there is nothing to consent to.
      </p>
      <p>Three things are kept in your browser's session storage:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          How far down each page you had scrolled, so the back button returns
          you where you were. This never leaves your device.
        </li>
        <li>
          Whether you have dismissed the prompt that appears once when leaving,
          so it does not appear again. This never leaves your device either.
        </li>
        <li>
          The random visit identifier described above, which is the one thing
          in this list that is sent to us.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        All three are cleared when you close the tab. None of them is a cookie,
        and none is readable by any other site.
      </p>

      <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
      <p>
        Page analytics are pruned after 90 days. The aggregate — which pages
        get read, which lead to enquiries — is what has value after that, and
        holding the raw rows longer is a liability rather than an asset.
      </p>
      <p>
        Enquiries are kept while the conversation is live and for as long as we
        may reasonably need them afterwards — a project has a history, and
        deleting the enquiry that started it makes that history incomplete.
        Records that went nowhere are reviewed and cleared periodically. If you
        want yours removed sooner, ask, and we will.
      </p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        You can ask us for a copy of what we hold about you, ask us to correct
        it, ask us to delete it, or object to us holding it at all. If you are
        in the UK or the EU, those rights come with a statutory deadline and we
        will answer inside it; if you are elsewhere, we will do the same thing
        anyway because there is no good reason not to.
      </p>
      <p>
        One email to{' '}
        <a href="mailto:contact@xtenzium.com">contact@xtenzium.com</a> is
        enough. We do not require a form, and we will not make it difficult.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are in the UK or the EU and think we have handled your data
        badly, you have the right to complain to your national data protection
        authority. We would rather you told us first so we can fix it.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, the date at the top of the page changes with it.
        A material change to how we handle enquiries will be described here
        rather than quietly folded in.
      </p>
    </LegalPage>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
